architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:939:## Wave 018 — Domain realization context boundary
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:1035:## Wave 019 — Three scene-host lifecycle and renderer platform boundary
architecture-audit/wave-plan.md:1039:Added by `cycle-018-wave-plan-extension` correction as the recommended next ordered wave after Wave 018. Requires the Cycle 018 wave-plan-extension review to sign off the ordering before any Wave 019 scout/proposal or implementation. Wave 019 must be re-read after Wave 018 completes; if Wave 018 changes realization/read-context assumptions materially, update this entry through a reviewed plan-edit mini-cycle before implementation.
architecture-audit/cycle-018-wave-plan-extension/plan-extension-note.md:38:Wave 019 is now added as the ordered next step after Wave 018: Three scene-host lifecycle and renderer platform boundary. This is ranked after Wave 018, not before it, because scene-host/runtime cleanup should consume stable typed realization/read-context outputs rather than compensating for full-`World`/`any` domain realization seams. It is ranked before app-shell service concentration, backend/frontend contract stance, validation-gate hardening, placeholder/debug cleanup, and docs/spec drift because current evidence shows the 3D rendering platform still owns central user-visible lifecycle concerns: `ThreeView.vue` wires resize/watch/world-change subscriptions, `ThreeViewRuntime` hard-wires the host/controller/adapters, and `ThreeSceneHost` owns camera/renderers/CSS2D DOM/OrbitControls/lights/animation/disposal in one class. App-shell and backend/docs cleanup are important, but they should not be pulled ahead of this remaining rendering-platform boundary unless Wave 018 invalidates the premise and a reviewed plan edit re-ranks again.
architecture-audit/cycle-018-wave-plan-extension/plan-extension-note.md:40:The proposed Wave 018 is bounded: it does not implement source changes in this stage, and its future implementation scope is limited to caller inventory, typed realization context contracts, presentation read-port adapter alignment, focused tests, and mandatory validation. Wave 019 is also bounded as planning only in this stage; its future implementation scope is limited to Three.js runtime/platform lifecycle seams, testable host/runtime/registry contracts, adapter disposal ownership, focused tests, and mandatory validation. Both entries explicitly reject broad app-shell, command/undo, persistence, backend, package/tooling, docs/spec, or validation-framework redesigns unless a reviewed plan edit broadens scope.
architecture-audit/cycle-018-wave-plan-extension/plan-extension-note.md:44:Run `cycle-018-wave-plan-extension-review` before any Cycle 018 scout/proposals or implementation. If the review signs off, start the canonical `cycle-018-fresh-scout-proposals` stage for Wave 018 — Domain realization context boundary — with scope check and direct/restructure proposals. Do not implement Wave 018 until proposal review convergence and decision are recorded. After Wave 018 completes and the plan is re-read, Wave 019 — Three scene-host lifecycle and renderer platform boundary — is the recommended next ordered candidate unless Wave 018 evidence requires a reviewed re-rank.
